WEBVTT

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:04.120
Local interactive inference has a different personality than server tuning.

00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:07.960
The user is usually waiting directly in front of the machine, so responsiveness matters.

00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:09.860
A coding assistant, research helper,

00:00:09.860 --> 00:00:13.450
or document analysis workflow may tolerate modest total response time,

00:00:13.450 --> 00:00:17.800
but it feels broken if the first token takes too long or generation stutters badly.

00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:21.080
For this environment, conservative settings are often the right starting point.

00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:24.620
Choose a batch size and microbatch size that leave memory headroom for the operating

00:00:24.620 --> 00:00:25.225
system,

00:00:25.225 --> 00:00:27.280
editor, browser, and other tools.

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:29.300
If the workload uses long-context prompts,

00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:32.520
test with realistic documents instead of short demos.

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:36.470
Long prompts can make a configuration that looked stable suddenly feel slow or fragile.

00:00:36.470 --> 00:00:38.745
Balance prompt speed against smooth generation.

00:00:38.745 --> 00:00:41.770
A larger batch setting may speed up ingestion of a long prompt,

00:00:41.770 --> 00:00:45.650
but if it causes memory pressure, thermal throttling, or uneven generation,

00:00:45.650 --> 00:00:47.315
the user experience may be worse.

00:00:47.315 --> 00:00:51.270
A smaller setting that streams reliably can be better for interactive work than a larger

00:00:51.270 --> 00:00:54.272
setting that wins one benchmark and then causes pauses.

00:00:54.272 --> 00:00:57.960
Document stable settings by model, quantization, context length, and runtime.

00:00:57.960 --> 00:00:59.630
A 7B model, a 13B model,

00:00:59.630 --> 00:01:04.160
and a larger mixture model can behave very differently on the same hardware.

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:06.220
Treat each configuration as its own operating profile.

00:01:06.220 --> 00:01:10.390
The result should be boring in the best way: predictable startup, predictable memory use,

00:01:10.390 --> 00:01:11.955
and predictable response behavior.

00:01:11.955 --> 00:01:15.160
Local users also notice interruptions that aggregate metrics hide.

00:01:15.160 --> 00:01:17.425
A benchmark may report an acceptable average,

00:01:17.425 --> 00:01:21.960
while the actual assistant pauses at awkward times or makes the editor feel sluggish.

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:23.930
Treat subjective responsiveness as evidence,

00:01:23.930 --> 00:01:27.940
then connect it back to measurements such as first-token latency, memory headroom,

00:01:27.940 --> 00:01:30.020
CPU load, and sustained token rate.
